fund-js/07-array-methods/01-multi-key-so.solution:
export function sortBy(rows, spec) {
  const keys = spec
    .split(',')
    .map((k) => k.trim())
    .filter(Boolean)
    .map((k) => (k.startsWith('-') ? { key: k.slice(1), dir: -1 } : { key: k, dir: 1 }));
  return rows.toSorted((a, b) => {
    for (const { key, dir } of keys) {
      if (a[key] < b[key]) return -dir;
      if (a[key] > b[key]) return dir;
    }
    return 0;
  });
}

fund-js/07-array-methods/01-multi-key-so.explanation:
Parse the spec once, then compare key by key and fall through on ties; `toSorted` copies and is stable:

```js
return rows.toSorted((a, b) => {
  for (const { key, dir } of keys) {
    if (a[key] < b[key]) return -dir;
    if (a[key] > b[key]) return dir;
  }
  return 0;
});
```

Returning 0 when every key ties is what keeps equal rows in their original order. `rows.sort(...)` would pass the ordering tests and fail the "input not reordered" one.

